# the argument of kant-groundwork-3.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
atom	canon	we must be able to will that a maxim of our action should be a universal law; this is the canon of the moral appreciation of the action
atom	inconceivable	some actions are such that their maxim cannot without contradiction be even conceived as a universal law of nature
atom	unwillable	in other actions it is impossible to will that their maxim should be raised to the universality of a law of nature, since such a will would contradict itself
atom	strict	the former actions violate strict or rigorous (inflexible) duty
atom	lax	the latter actions violate only laxer (meritorious) duty
atom	same_principle	all duties depend as regards the nature of the obligation on the same principle
# "must be able to will" is kept inside the atom canon rather than written in kd, since the argument does not turn on the modal step.
P1	S2	canon	kind normative
P2	S4	inconceivable
P3	S5	unwillable
P4	S5	inconceivable → strict
P5	S5	unwillable → lax
C1	S5	strict ∧ lax	from P2 P3 P4 P5
# The text's "thus" relies on this link between the canon and both classes of duty; S1 says the duties fall into two classes on the one principle.
A1	-	canon ∧ strict ∧ lax → same_principle
C	S6	same_principle	from C1 P1 A1
